{"answer":"The critic's claim that the wrapper introduces a net negative for small teams relies on the assumption that the 'integration tax' is inherently prohibitive, yet the target proposal explicitly conditions adoption on this tax remaining below 40% of the development time for a custom build. If this threshold is exceeded, the proposal itself dictates flipping to a custom build, thereby negating the risk of excessive overhead. Furthermore, the critic conflates the 'system of record' with the 'system of control'; the mechanism specifically decouples these to avoid the 'migration cliff' and single points of failure associated with monolithic custom trackers, rather than creating new fragility. The argument that operational overhead exceeds development overhead ignores the scenario where the existing tool's API supports real-time parameter injection, which is a stated prerequisite; if the tool is read-only, the proposal already mandates a full custom build, rendering the 'wrapper' strategy inapplicable and immune to the critic's specific latency and fragility objections in those cases."}